    "value_a": 2,
    "value_b": 1,
    "w_confidence": 0.6,
    "w_value": 0.4,
    "conf_floor": 40.0,
    "_notes": {
      "_what": "CHOSEN, not measured. Everything under backtest.calibration was fitted from real games; these were decided. Kept side by side so which is which is obvious.",
      "conf_a": "calibrated probability, in percent, needed for an A on confidence",
      "value_a": "points of calibrated probability above the price's break-even needed for an A on value",
      "w_confidence": "how much the merged grade leans on confidence rather than value",
      "conf_floor": "calibrated probability, in percent, below which a market cannot grade above D however good the price is. CHOSEN, and provisional: on the week 1 slate any value from 40 to 50 demotes the same seven moneylines, because the calls sit at 8-38% and then jump to 52%, so the number is not perched on a cliff edge. It is not yet measured. cfb_backtest.py should test whether the calibration holds in the tail and set this from evidence."
    }
